Chapter: DtlsMessageTest
  cites Foreword chapter Aes
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Encode chapter DtlsMessage

 The DTLS 1.3 handshake message layer (RFC 9147 5.2 and 7).

 The load-bearing assertion in this file is the transcript rule. RFC 9147
 says the transcript is computed over the TLS 1.3-style message WITHOUT
 message_seq, fragment_offset and fragment_length -- so the wire form and
 the transcript form of the SAME message are different bytes, and the
 test asserts they are different rather than assuming it. An
 implementation that hashes what it sent is self-consistent, passes every
 round-trip test anyone would write, and cannot complete a handshake with
 any other implementation on earth.

 The finished_key is gated on RFC 8448's published value -- the label and
 the empty context are exactly the sort of thing that is wrong silently.

Section: Framing

  m-body : List Integer = [222, 173, 190, 239]

  m-wire : List Integer = dtls-msg-encode dtls-hs-client-hello 7 m-body
  m-tr : List Integer = tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-client-hello m-body

  x-hex : List Integer -> Text
  x-hex (bs) = x-hex-loop bs 0 (list-length bs) ""

  x-hex-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  x-hex-loop (bs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else x-hex-loop bs (i + 1) n (acc & aes-hex-byte (list-at bs i))

  test-wire : Text =
    "dm wire=" & x-hex m-wire

 The same message, transcript form. Twelve bytes of header on the wire;
 four in the hash.

  test-transcript : Text =
    "dm transcript=" & x-hex m-tr

 And the assertion that matters: these are NOT the same bytes. If this
 ever reads same=True, someone has "simplified" the transcript to reuse
 the wire encoding and the handshake will never interoperate again.

  test-differ : Text =
    "dm same=" & show (tls13-ct-equal m-wire m-tr) & " wirelen=" & show (list-length m-wire) & " trlen=" & show (list-length m-tr)

Section: Parse

  test-parse : Text =
    when dtls-msg-parse m-wire
      is None -> "dm parse=REJECTED"
      is Just (m) -> "dm parse type=" & show (m.dm-type) & " len=" & show (m.dm-length) & " seq=" & show (m.dm-seq) & " foff=" & show (m.dm-frag-offset) & " flen=" & show (m.dm-frag-length) & " body=" & x-hex (m.dm-body)

 A truncated header is not a message. A datagram protocol is handed
 rubbish constantly, and reading past the end of it is the attack.

  test-short : Text =
    when dtls-msg-parse [1, 0, 0]
      is None -> "dm short=REJECTED"
      is Just (m) -> "dm short=ACCEPTED"

 A header whose fragment_length runs past the datagram is a lie. Trusting
 it is an out-of-bounds read.

  test-liar : Text =
    when dtls-msg-parse [1, 0, 0, 99, 0, 7, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 99, 1, 2]
      is None -> "dm liar=REJECTED"
      is Just (m) -> "dm liar=ACCEPTED"

 A parsed message must hash to the same transcript bytes the sender put
 in. This is the round trip that matters: not wire-to-wire, but
 wire-to-TRANSCRIPT.

  test-roundtrip : Text =
    when dtls-msg-parse m-wire
      is None -> "dm rt=REJECTED"
      is Just (m) -> "dm rt=" & show (tls13-ct-equal (dtls-msg-to-transcript m) m-tr)

Section: Finished

 RFC 8448's published finished_key for the server handshake traffic
 secret. If the label or the empty context is wrong, this line is wrong
 and everything derived from it is wrong.

  s-hs : List Integer =
    [182, 123, 125, 105, 12, 193, 108, 78, 117, 229, 66, 19, 203, 45, 55, 180,
     233, 201, 18, 188, 222, 217, 16, 93, 66, 190, 253, 89, 211, 145, 173, 56]

  test-finished-key : Text =
    "dm finished-key=" & x-hex (tls13-finished-key s-hs)

 verify_data = HMAC(finished_key, transcript_hash).

  t-msg : List Integer =
    [99, 111, 100, 101, 120, 32, 100, 116, 108, 115, 32, 116, 114, 97, 110, 115, 99, 114, 105, 112, 116]

  t-hash : List Integer = tls13-transcript-hash t-msg

  test-transcript-hash : Text =
    "dm t-hash=" & x-hex t-hash

  test-verify-data : Text =
    "dm verify=" & x-hex (tls13-verify-data s-hs t-hash)

 A Finished that verifies proves the peer holds the same handshake
 secret. It does NOT prove who the peer is -- there is still no peer
 authentication anywhere in this stack. A wrong transcript must fail.

  test-finished-ok : Text =
    "dm fin-ok=" & show (tls13-finished-ok s-hs t-hash (tls13-verify-data s-hs t-hash))

  test-finished-bad : Text =
    "dm fin-bad=" & show (tls13-finished-ok s-hs (tls13-transcript-hash [1, 2, 3]) (tls13-verify-data s-hs t-hash))

Section: ACK

 RFC 9147 section 7: a vector of record numbers, each a uint64 epoch and
 a uint64 sequence number, preceded by its length in bytes.

  acks : List DtlsRecordNum =
    [DtlsRecordNum { rn-epoch = 3, rn-seq = 4660 }, DtlsRecordNum { rn-epoch = 3, rn-seq = 4661 }]

  test-ack : Text =
    "dm ack=" & x-hex (dtls-ack-encode acks) & " count=" & show (dtls-ack-count (dtls-ack-encode acks))

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni test-wire
    print-line-uni test-transcript
    print-line-uni test-differ
    print-line-uni test-parse
    print-line-uni test-short
    print-line-uni test-liar
    print-line-uni test-roundtrip
    print-line-uni test-finished-key
    print-line-uni test-transcript-hash
    print-line-uni test-verify-data
    print-line-uni test-finished-ok
    print-line-uni test-finished-bad
    print-line-uni test-ack
  end
